WEBVTT

00:00.540 --> 00:04.150
Part C, find the
particular solution--

00:04.150 --> 00:06.696
W as a function of t-- to
the differential equations,

00:06.696 --> 00:08.070
derivative of W
with respect to t

00:08.070 --> 00:11.670
is equal to 1 over
25 times w minus 300,

00:11.670 --> 00:15.660
with initial condition, at
time 0, W is equal to 1,400.

00:15.660 --> 00:21.460
And the units in this problem,
it was 1,400 tons they told us.

00:21.460 --> 00:24.560
So it might seem strange to
see a differential equations

00:24.560 --> 00:26.330
problem on an AP exam.

00:26.330 --> 00:27.970
You say, wait, this isn't fair.

00:27.970 --> 00:30.926
This isn't a differential
equations class.

00:30.926 --> 00:33.050
And the thing to keep in
mind is if, on an AP exam,

00:33.050 --> 00:34.170
you see differential
equations, they're

00:34.170 --> 00:36.461
really asking you to solve
a differential equation that

00:36.461 --> 00:38.640
can be solved using
the skills that you

00:38.640 --> 00:40.730
would learn in an AP class.

00:40.730 --> 00:43.516
And the main class of
differential equations

00:43.516 --> 00:44.890
that you could
solve are the ones

00:44.890 --> 00:46.640
that are separable
differential equations.

00:46.640 --> 00:48.690
The ones where you can
separate the independent

00:48.690 --> 00:50.331
and the dependent variables.

00:50.331 --> 00:52.580
And if you don't have any
idea what I'm talking about,

00:52.580 --> 00:53.496
I'm about to show you.

00:53.496 --> 00:56.030
So let me rewrite this
differential equation.

00:56.030 --> 01:04.069
We have dw, dt is equal to
1 over 25 times W minus 300.

01:04.069 --> 01:06.300
Now this differential
equation, we have W here.

01:06.300 --> 01:07.790
And we have this dt.

01:07.790 --> 01:09.700
But we don't have
t anywhere else.

01:09.700 --> 01:12.370
So what I want to do is I want
to get the parts that involve W

01:12.370 --> 01:14.590
onto the left-hand
side, and I want

01:14.590 --> 01:18.020
to get anything that deals
with dt on the right-hand side.

01:18.020 --> 01:23.740
So let's divide both sides of
this equation by w minus 300.

01:23.740 --> 01:29.417
So we would have 1
over W minus 300 dw

01:29.417 --> 01:31.250
dt-- the derivative of
W with respect to t--

01:31.250 --> 01:34.880
is equal to-- I divide
this side by W minus 300,

01:34.880 --> 01:37.710
so it's equal to 1 over 25.

01:37.710 --> 01:40.050
Now I want to get the
dt's on this side.

01:40.050 --> 01:42.930
So let's multiply
both sides by dt.

01:42.930 --> 01:44.430
[INAUDIBLE] the
differential is just

01:44.430 --> 01:47.210
a very, very small change in t.

01:47.210 --> 01:51.620
And so we get-- and I'm a little
bit imprecise with my notation

01:51.620 --> 01:52.120
here.

01:52.120 --> 01:53.970
But this is one way that
you can think about it when

01:53.970 --> 01:55.928
you are dealing with the
differential equations

01:55.928 --> 01:58.040
like this, separable
differential equations.

01:58.040 --> 02:03.860
You have 1 over W minus
300 dw-- let me make sure

02:03.860 --> 02:06.600
that the dw doesn't look like
it's in the denominator--

02:06.600 --> 02:13.730
dw is equal to 1 over 25 dt.

02:13.730 --> 02:16.830
And now we can integrate both
sides, which is essentially

02:16.830 --> 02:20.200
just saying that we're taking--
since this is equal to this--

02:20.200 --> 02:22.950
that each little
increment of W times this

02:22.950 --> 02:25.960
is equal to each little
increment of dt times 1

02:25.960 --> 02:27.340
over 25.

02:27.340 --> 02:31.050
We can then say, well, if we sum
up all of the increments of W

02:31.050 --> 02:33.360
times this quantity--
if we sum up

02:33.360 --> 02:35.970
the infinite number of
infinitesimally small

02:35.970 --> 02:38.320
increments-- that would be
the same thing as summing up

02:38.320 --> 02:39.480
all of these characters.

02:39.480 --> 02:41.700
Because each of these
characters is really

02:41.700 --> 02:44.260
the same as each of
these characters.

02:44.260 --> 02:45.390
So let's do that sum.

02:45.390 --> 02:49.360
We're essentially just
integrating both sides of this.

02:49.360 --> 02:52.260
And here, you might be able
to do this in your head.

02:52.260 --> 02:54.490
Or we could do the
formal u substitution,

02:54.490 --> 02:56.320
just to make it clear
what we're doing.

02:56.320 --> 03:02.010
If you said u is equal to W
minus 300, then what is du?

03:02.010 --> 03:03.410
If you wanted the differential.

03:03.410 --> 03:05.159
So if you want to take
the derivative of u

03:05.159 --> 03:09.660
with respect to W, you would
say du with respect to W

03:09.660 --> 03:11.840
is equal to what?

03:11.840 --> 03:13.150
It is equal to 1.

03:13.150 --> 03:15.590
The derivative of this
with respect to W is 1.

03:15.590 --> 03:18.630
Or if you multiply
both sides times dw,

03:18.630 --> 03:22.440
you have du is equal to dw.

03:22.440 --> 03:26.290
And so this left-hand side right
over here-- this integral--

03:26.290 --> 03:32.490
could be rewritten as the
integral of 1 over u du.

03:32.490 --> 03:34.190
We define this as u.

03:34.190 --> 03:36.610
And we just saw that du
is the same thing as dw,

03:36.610 --> 03:38.560
so this could be
rewritten as du.

03:38.560 --> 03:40.540
And this is one of
the basic integrals,

03:40.540 --> 03:41.910
so hopefully we've learned.

03:41.910 --> 03:46.230
And this is equal to the natural
log of the absolute value of u.

03:46.230 --> 03:48.680
Or if you unwind
the substitution,

03:48.680 --> 03:51.820
this is equal to the natural
log of the absolute value

03:51.820 --> 03:55.460
of W minus 300.

03:55.460 --> 03:56.697
So let me write this down.

03:56.697 --> 03:58.280
So the left-hand
side right over here.

03:58.280 --> 03:59.210
And you might be able
do that in your head.

03:59.210 --> 04:00.001
You say, hey, look.

04:00.001 --> 04:01.190
You have W minus 300.

04:01.190 --> 04:03.840
Its derivative is 1, so
it's essentially there.

04:03.840 --> 04:06.660
So I can just take this
as if this was 1 over-- I

04:06.660 --> 04:09.320
could just take the
antiderivative here and treat

04:09.320 --> 04:11.235
this whole thing as a variable.

04:11.235 --> 04:13.610
So it would just be the natural
log of this entire thing,

04:13.610 --> 04:15.910
or the absolute value
of that entire thing.

04:15.910 --> 04:18.480
So the integral of
the left-hand side

04:18.480 --> 04:24.496
is the natural log of the
absolute value of W minus 300.

04:24.496 --> 04:26.662
And the derivative of the
right-hand side-- and this

04:26.662 --> 04:30.360
is a little bit more
straightforward-- or sorry,

04:30.360 --> 04:33.170
the antiderivative with respect
to t on the right-hand side

04:33.170 --> 04:39.045
is 1 over 25 t, or we
could say t over 25.

04:42.090 --> 04:44.160
And then these are both
indefinite integrals.

04:44.160 --> 04:46.460
You could have a constant
on both sides or one side.

04:46.460 --> 04:48.501
We could just put it on
one side right over here.

04:48.501 --> 04:50.440
You could have put
plus some constant.

04:50.440 --> 04:52.635
You could have added
some C1 over here.

04:52.635 --> 04:54.260
And then you could've
had C2 over here.

04:54.260 --> 04:56.426
But then you could've
subtracted C1 from both sides,

04:56.426 --> 04:59.094
and then just have a
C3 constant over here.

04:59.094 --> 05:00.510
But to simplify
it, you could just

05:00.510 --> 05:04.130
say, look, this is going
to be equal to this

05:04.130 --> 05:05.465
plus some constant.

05:05.465 --> 05:07.590
You could've put the constant
on that side as well.

05:07.590 --> 05:08.340
It doesn't matter.

05:08.340 --> 05:10.860
All the constants
would merge into one.

05:10.860 --> 05:13.150
Now we've gotten pretty far.

05:13.150 --> 05:16.950
We want to solve the
particular solution.

05:16.950 --> 05:18.760
Right now, we only
have this constant.

05:18.760 --> 05:22.350
And we also haven't written W as
a function of t just right yet.

05:22.350 --> 05:24.580
They're implicitly expressed.

05:24.580 --> 05:27.710
It is more of a relationship
than a function right now.

05:27.710 --> 05:29.480
So the first thing
we can do is try

05:29.480 --> 05:36.140
to solve for C. We're told
in the problem that W of 0

05:36.140 --> 05:40.590
is equal to 1400 tons.

05:40.590 --> 05:41.410
They told us that.

05:41.410 --> 05:43.610
That's one of our
initial conditions.

05:43.610 --> 05:49.010
So we know that the natural
log, that when t is equal to 0,

05:49.010 --> 05:50.990
W is equal to 1400.

05:50.990 --> 06:00.450
So W is equal to 1,400 when
t is equal to 0 plus C.

06:00.450 --> 06:02.580
Or another way to
think about it is,

06:02.580 --> 06:08.314
the natural log of the absolute
value of 1,100 is equal to C.

06:08.314 --> 06:09.980
And obviously, this
is a positive value.

06:09.980 --> 06:13.370
So we could just say that C
is equal to the natural log

06:13.370 --> 06:14.250
of 1,100.

06:14.250 --> 06:15.830
We can drop the
absolute value sign

06:15.830 --> 06:18.990
because the absolute
value of 1,100 is 1,100.

06:18.990 --> 06:22.270
So now we can rewrite
this part right over here.

06:22.270 --> 06:23.940
Let me do it in yellow.

06:23.940 --> 06:26.790
Let me start--
that's not yellow.

06:26.790 --> 06:28.090
Let me start right over here.

06:28.090 --> 06:31.050
So we could write
the natural log

06:31.050 --> 06:34.990
of the absolute
value of W minus 300.

06:34.990 --> 06:38.040
And actually, we know that
W is an increasing function.

06:38.040 --> 06:41.440
W starts at 1,400 and
only goes up from there

06:41.440 --> 06:43.450
as t gets larger and larger.

06:43.450 --> 06:45.500
So if we're starting at
1,400 and only getting

06:45.500 --> 06:48.420
larger and larger values
for W, this expression right

06:48.420 --> 06:53.180
here is never going to get
negative for positive t values.

06:53.180 --> 06:54.534
And that's all we care about.

06:54.534 --> 06:56.700
And so we can actually drop
the absolute value sign.

06:56.700 --> 06:58.440
This will always be
a positive value.

06:58.440 --> 07:04.580
So we could say the absolute
value of W minus 300

07:04.580 --> 07:07.380
is equal to t over
25-- and we've just

07:07.380 --> 07:12.590
solved for our constant--
plus the natural log of 1,100.

07:12.590 --> 07:15.030
And now we can just solve
this explicitly for W.

07:15.030 --> 07:18.900
We can raise e to both sides.

07:18.900 --> 07:20.855
This value is equal
to this value.

07:20.855 --> 07:22.980
The left hand side is equal
to the right hand side.

07:22.980 --> 07:25.370
So e raised to this power
is the same thing as e

07:25.370 --> 07:27.150
raised to that power.

07:27.150 --> 07:32.400
So we have e to the
natural log of W minus 300

07:32.400 --> 07:36.310
is going to be equal
to e to the t over 25

07:36.310 --> 07:39.600
plus the natural log of 1,100.

07:39.600 --> 07:44.050
Let me take this-- actually,
just to get the real estate,

07:44.050 --> 07:47.020
let me delete that.

07:47.020 --> 07:47.520
All right.

07:47.520 --> 07:49.830
Now I can keep working
right below that.

07:49.830 --> 07:52.060
And so e to the natural
log of something

07:52.060 --> 07:53.930
is just going to be
equal to that something.

07:53.930 --> 07:56.013
The natural log is what
power do I have to raise e

07:56.013 --> 07:57.850
to to get this thing.

07:57.850 --> 07:59.340
Well I'm raising
e to that power,

07:59.340 --> 08:00.640
so I'm going to get this thing.

08:00.640 --> 08:04.510
So the left-hand side
just becomes W minus 300,

08:04.510 --> 08:07.475
and the right-hand side,
we could rewrite this as e

08:07.475 --> 08:14.690
to the t over 25 times e to
the natural log of 1,100.

08:14.690 --> 08:17.352
We have the same base,
different exponents.

08:17.352 --> 08:18.810
If you wanted to
simplify this, you

08:18.810 --> 08:20.268
would just add the
exponents, which

08:20.268 --> 08:21.730
is exactly what we did up here.

08:21.730 --> 08:24.380
So these two things
are equivalent.

08:24.380 --> 08:26.820
e to the natural log of
1,100, that is 1,100.

08:26.820 --> 08:28.780
The natural log of
1,100 is a power

08:28.780 --> 08:31.280
that you raise e
to to get to 1,100.

08:31.280 --> 08:34.669
So if you raise e to that
power, you get 1,100.

08:34.669 --> 08:36.409
So that is 1,100.

08:36.409 --> 08:46.780
So we get W minus 300 is equal
to 1,100 e to the t over 25.

08:46.780 --> 08:49.320
And then you just add
300 to both sides.

08:49.320 --> 08:56.220
W is equal to 1,100 e to
the t over 25 plus 300.

08:56.220 --> 08:57.430
And we're done.

08:57.430 --> 08:59.400
We have solved the
differential equation.

08:59.400 --> 09:01.400
We found the
particular solution.

09:01.400 --> 09:04.170
We had to solve for C to find
that particular solution.

09:04.170 --> 09:04.670
We're done.

09:04.670 --> 09:06.590
And we did it using
just basic integration.

09:06.590 --> 09:09.330
We didn't have to use any
of the fancy differential

09:09.330 --> 09:11.690
equation solving
tools that you might

09:11.690 --> 09:14.690
learn in a more advanced class.
